  add('Application','A24','Observed values are \\(100,121,143,174\\). Assess whether \\(u_n=100(1.2)^{n-1}\\) is exact or approximate.','Approximate, not exact.','The model values are \\(100,120,144,172.8\\); small residuals remain, so the ratio \\(1.2\\) is a plausible approximation.',3,'GDC allowed','Assess');

  // Reasoning · 24
  add('Reasoning','R01','Explain why \\(4,12,36,108,\\ldots\\) is geometric.','Every consecutive ratio is \\(3\\).','A valid explanation computes at least two ratios and identifies the same multiplier.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R02','Prove that \\(u_n=7(1.4)^{n-1}\\) is geometric.','\\(u_{n+1}/u_n=1.4\\).','\\(u_{n+1}/u_n=[7(1.4)^n]/[7(1.4)^{n-1}]=1.4\\), independent of \\(n\\).',3,'GDC allowed','Prove');
  add('Reasoning','R03','Explain why the exponent in \\(u_n=u_1r^{n-1}\\) is \\(n-1\\).','Term \\(1\\) is the starting value, so reaching term \\(n\\) requires \\(n-1\\) multiplications.','Substituting \\(n=1\\) must return \\(u_1\\), which requires exponent \\(0\\).',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R04','Explain why a decreasing positive sequence can have a positive common ratio.','A ratio between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) preserves sign but decreases magnitude.','For example, multiplying by \\(0.8\\) keeps values positive while reducing them.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R05','Explain how \\(r=-0.6\\) affects signs and magnitudes.','Signs alternate and magnitudes decrease toward zero.','The negative sign flips every term; \\(|r|=0.6<1\\) creates decay.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R06','Given positive separated terms with an even index gap, explain why a negative ratio may be possible.','Even powers remove the sign of \\(r\\).','If \\(u_q/u_p=r^{2k}>0\\), both positive and negative roots may satisfy the separated-term condition.',3,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R07','Show that consecutive non-zero geometric terms \\(A,B,C\\) satisfy \\(B^2=AC\\).','Let \\(B=Ar\\) and \\(C=Ar^2\\); both sides equal \\(A^2r^2\\).','This proves the geometric-mean relation.',3,'GDC allowed','Show');
  add('Reasoning','R08','The sequence \\(1,4,9,16,\\ldots\\) is not geometric. Explain.','Its ratios are \\(4,9/4,16/9\\), not constant.','Visual growth alone is insufficient; the ratio test fails.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R09','Derive \\(S_n=u_1(1-r^n)/(1-r)\\) for \\(r\\neq1\\).','Multiply the sum by \\(r\\), align, subtract and divide by \\(1-r\\).','The interior terms cancel, leaving \\((1-r)S_n=u_1(1-r^n)\\).',4,'GDC allowed','Derive');
  add('Reasoning','R10','Explain why the finite-sum formula with denominator \\(1-r\\) is not used directly when \\(r=1\\).','The denominator is zero; the sequence is constant and \\(S_n=nu_1\\).','The formula has a removable structural edge case but cannot be evaluated by division by zero.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R11','Explain why \\(\\sum_{k=5}^{20}u_k\\) contains \\(16\\) terms.','Both endpoints are included: \\(20-5+1=16\\).','Counting gaps gives \\(15\\), while counting index values gives \\(16\\).',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R12','A threshold calculation gives \\(n>12.3\\). Explain why the first admissible stage is \\(13\\), not \\(12\\).','Stages are positive integers and stage \\(12\\) does not satisfy the inequality.','Verify the original model at stages \\(12\\) and \\(13\\).',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R13','For a decay model with \\(0<r<1\\), explain why solving a logarithmic inequality requires care.','Because \\(\\log r<0\\), dividing by it reverses the inequality.','A table or direct solver plus adjacent-term verification can reduce sign errors.',3,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R14','A student reports the real-valued threshold solution \\(n=8.41\\). Diagnose the error.','A sequence index must be a positive integer; the student must test \\(n=9\\) and the adjacent stage.','The continuous boundary is not itself an admissible sequence stage.',2,'GDC allowed','Correct');
  add('Reasoning','R15','Explain why three \\(10\\%\\) increases produce a \\(33.1\\%\\) increase, not \\(30\\%\\).','The factor is applied to a changing base: \\(1.1^3=1.331\\).','Percentage increases compound multiplicatively.',3,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R16','A model uses \\(r=1.25\\). Interpret the common ratio and the changing absolute differences.','The value increases \\(25\\%\\) each step; absolute increases become larger.','The same relative change acts on an increasing base.',3,'GDC allowed','Interpret');
  add('Reasoning','R17','Show that every second term of a geometric sequence is geometric and state its ratio.','The ratio is \\(r^2\\).','\\(u_{n+2}/u_n=r^2\\), so selecting indices two apart creates a geometric subsequence.',3,'GDC allowed','Show');
  add('Reasoning','R18','For \\(u_1=4,r=3\\), find the sum of \\(u_1+u_3+u_5+u_7\\) and explain the new ratio.','\\(3280\\); the selected terms have ratio \\(r^2=9\\).','The terms are \\(4,36,324,2916\\), whose sum is \\(3280\\).',4,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:3280,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Reasoning','R19','For \\(u_1=4,r=3\\), find \\(u_2+u_4+u_6+u_8\\).','\\(9840\\).','The selected terms are \\(12,108,972,8748\\), a geometric sequence with ratio \\(9\\).',4,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:9840,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Reasoning','R20','Two geometric models are \\(A_n=50(1.2)^{n-1}\\) and \\(B_n=80(1.1)^{n-1}\\). Explain how to find the first stage at which \\(A_n>B_n\\).','Solve \\((1.2/1.1)^{n-1}>80/50\\), then choose and verify the first integer stage.','Dividing the models creates a single exponential inequality.',4,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R21','Observed ratios are \\(1.19,1.21,1.18\\). Assess the claim that the data are exactly geometric with \\(r=1.2\\).','The model is approximate, not exact.','The ratios are close to \\(1.2\\) but not identical; measurement variation or changing conditions remain.',3,'GDC allowed','Assess');
  add('Reasoning','R22','State two reasons a fixed-ratio population model may fail over a long period.','Examples: limited resources, carrying capacity, migration, intervention or changing reproduction rates.','Each reason challenges the assumption of a constant multiplier under unchanged conditions.',3,'GDC allowed','State');
  add('Reasoning','R23','Explain why a bouncing-ball distance formula counts each rebound height twice but the initial height once.','Each rebound height is travelled upward and downward; the initial height is only the first downward drop.','This prevents a common double-counting or omission error.',3,'GDC allowed','Explain');
  add('Reasoning','R24','Explain why the infinite-sum formula is not part of the core finite-series calculation in this lesson.','Core SL 1.3 assesses finite terms and sums; an infinite sum additionally requires a convergence condition \\(|r|<1\\).','The extension changes both the number of terms and the mathematical condition required.',2,'GDC allowed','Explain');

  // Challenge · 24
  add('Challenge','C01','In a geometric sequence, \\(u_4=54\\) and \\(u_7=1458\\). Find \\(u_1\\) and \\(r\\).','\\(u_1=2,r=3\\).','\\(r^3=1458/54=27\\Rightarrow r=3\\). Then \\(u_1=54/3^3=2\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'text',accepted:['u1=2,r=3','2,3']}});
  add('Challenge','C02','In a geometric sequence, \\(u_2=18\\) and \\(u_4=2\\). Find every real pair \\((u_1,r)\\).','\\((54,1/3),(-54,-1/3))\\).','\\(r^2=2/18=1/9\\), so \\(r=\\pm1/3\\). Then \\(u_1=18/r\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find');
  add('Challenge','C03','The first three terms are \\(x,x+6,4x\\). Find every real \\(x\\) for which they form a non-zero geometric sequence.','\\(x=6\\) or \\(x=-2\\).','Use \\((x+6)^2=4x^2\\), giving \\(x^2-4x-12=0\\).',4,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'text',accepted:['6,-2','x=6 or -2','-2,6']}});
  add('Challenge','C04','The first three terms are \\(2p-1,3p+2,8p-5\\). Find all real values of \\(p\\) that make them geometric.','\\(p=1\\) or \\(p=-\\frac{13}{7}\\).','Set \\((3p+2)^2=(2p-1)(8p-5)\\), simplify to \\(7p^2+6p-13=0=(p-1)(7p+13)\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find');
  add('Challenge','C05','A geometric sequence has \\(u_3+u_4=36\\) and \\(u_5+u_6=324\\). Find all possible real ratios.','\\(r=3\\) or \\(r=-3\\), subject to non-zero denominators.','The second pair equals \\(r^2\\) times the first pair, so \\(r^2=324/36=9\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find');
  add('Challenge','C06','A geometric sequence has \\(u_1=3\\) and \\(S_5=93\\). Find the positive ratio.','\\(r=2\\).','\\(93=3(1-r^5)/(1-r)\\), and \\(1+r+r^2+r^3+r^4=31\\); the positive solution is \\(2\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'number',value:2,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C07','A geometric sequence has \\(u_4=40\\) and \\(S_4=75\\). Find \\(u_1\\) and \\(r\\).','\\(u_1=5,r=2\\).','Let \\(u_1=a\\). Then \\(ar^3=40\\) and \\(a(1+r+r^2+r^3)=75\\). The consistent positive solution is \\(a=5,r=2\\).',6,'GDC allowed','Determine');
  add('Challenge','C08','Find the least \\(n\\) for which \\(5+7.5+11.25+\\cdots\\) has \\(S_n>1000\\).','\\(n=12\\).','\\(S_n=10(1.5^n-1)\\). Check \\(S_{11}\\approx854.98\\) and \\(S_{12}\\approx1287.46\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'number',value:12,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C09','For \\(u_1=100,r=0.9\\), find the least \\(n\\) for which the finite sum exceeds \\(800\\).','\\(n=16\\).','\\(S_n=1000(1-0.9^n)\\). Check \\(S_{15}\\approx794.11\\) and \\(S_{16}\\approx814.70\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'number',value:16,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C10','For \\(u_1=2,r=3\\), calculate \\(u_4+u_5+\\cdots+u_{12}\\).','\\(531360\\).','The block has \\(9\\) terms, first \\(54\\), ratio \\(3\\): \\(54(3^9-1)/2=531360\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:531360,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C11','For \\(u_1=5,r=-2\\), calculate the sum of the odd-index terms from \\(u_1\\) to \\(u_9\\).','\\(1705\\).','Odd-index terms are \\(5,20,80,320,1280\\), ratio \\(4\\), sum \\(1705\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:1705,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C12','For \\(u_1=5,r=-2\\), calculate the sum of the even-index terms from \\(u_2\\) to \\(u_{10}\\).','\\(-3410\\).','Even-index terms are \\(-10,-40,-160,-640,-2560\\), ratio \\(4\\), sum \\(-3410\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:-3410,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C13','A ball is dropped from \\(12\\) m and rebounds to \\(65\\%\\) of each previous height. Find the total distance after \\(6\\) complete rebounds.','\\(52.7179\\) m approximately.','\\(D=12+24\\sum_{k=1}^{6}0.65^k\\approx52.7179\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:52.7179,tolerance:0.002}});
  add('Challenge','C14','Two models are \\(A_n=50(1.2)^{n-1}\\) and \\(B_n=80(1.1)^{n-1}\\). Find the first stage at which \\(A_n>B_n\\).','\\(n=7\\).','Solve \\((1.2/1.1)^{n-1}>1.6\\). Check \\(A_6\\approx124.42<B_6\\approx128.84\\), while \\(A_7\\approx149.30>B_7\\approx141.72\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Determine',{check:{mode:'number',value:7,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C15','A sequence is geometric with positive ratio. Given \\(S_3=21\\) and \\(S_6=189\\), find \\(u_1\\) and \\(r\\).','\\(u_1=3,r=2\\).','The next three-term block is \\(S_6-S_3=168=r^3S_3\\), so \\(r^3=8\\Rightarrow r=2\\). Then \\(u_1(1+2+4)=21\\Rightarrow u_1=3\\).',6,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'text',accepted:['u1=3,r=2','3,2']}});
  add('Challenge','C16','A geometric sequence has positive ratio and \\(u_2+u_3=30\\), \\(u_4+u_5=270\\). Find \\(r\\) and \\(u_1\\).','\\(r=3,u_1=2.5\\).','The second pair is \\(r^2\\) times the first: \\(r^2=9\\Rightarrow r=3\\). Then \\(u_1r(1+r)=30\\Rightarrow12u_1=30\\).',6,'GDC allowed','Find');
  add('Challenge','C17','A model predicts \\(200(1.15)^{n-1}\\). Observed values for stages \\(1\\)–\\(4\\) are \\(202,228,266,301\\). Calculate multiplicative residuals observed/predicted and comment.','Approximately \\(1.010,0.991,1.006,0.990\\); the model is close but not exact.','Predictions are \\(200,230,264.5,304.175\\). Ratios near \\(1\\) indicate small relative discrepancies.',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate');
  add('Challenge','C18','A growth model begins at \\(500\\) with ratio \\(1.3\\), but from stage \\(6\\) onward the ratio becomes \\(1.1\\). Find stage \\(8\\).','\\(u_8=500(1.3)^4(1.1)^3\\approx1901.46\\).','Stages \\(1\\) to \\(5\\) use four factors of \\(1.3\\); stages \\(5\\) to \\(8\\) use three factors of \\(1.1\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Calculate',{check:{mode:'number',value:1901.46,tolerance:0.03}});
  add('Challenge','C19','A geometric series has first term \\(a>0\\), ratio \\(r>0\\), \\(u_5=48\\) and \\(S_5=93\\). Find \\(a\\) and \\(r\\).','\\(a=3,r=2\\).','\\(ar^4=48\\) and \\(a(1+r+r^2+r^3+r^4)=93\\). The positive solution is \\(a=3,r=2\\).',6,'GDC allowed','Determine');
  add('Challenge','C20','Find all real \\(r\\) such that \\(1,r,r^2,r^3\\) has sum \\(0\\).','\\(r=-1\\).','\\(1+r+r^2+r^3=(1+r)(1+r^2)\\). The only real root is \\(r=-1\\).',4,'GDC allowed','Find',{check:{mode:'number',value:-1,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C21','A geometric sequence has \\(u_1=4\\), \\(r=1.5\\). Find the first \\(n\\) for which \\(u_n\\) exceeds the cumulative total of the first \\(n-2\\) terms.','\\(n=5\\).','Check \\(u_n=4(1.5)^{n-1}\\) against \\(S_{n-2}=8(1.5^{n-2}-1)\\). Direct testing gives first validity at \\(n=5\\): \\(u_5=20.25>S_3=19\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Determine',{check:{mode:'number',value:5,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C22','Show that if \\(u_p,u_q,u_r\\) are terms of a positive geometric sequence and \\(p+r=2q\\), then \\(u_q^2=u_pu_r\\).','Use \\(u_k=u_1a^{k-1}\\); the exponents on both sides are equal.','\\(u_q^2=u_1^2a^{2q-2}\\), while \\(u_pu_r=u_1^2a^{p+r-2}=u_1^2a^{2q-2}\\).',5,'GDC allowed','Show');
  add('Challenge','C23','A value is modeled by \\(u_n=900(0.72)^{n-1}\\). Find the first stage below \\(50\\), and state one reason the model may fail near zero.','\\(n=10\\); measurement floors or a non-zero background can invalidate the model.','\\(u_9\\approx65.20\\), \\(u_{10}\\approx46.94\\). Real measurements may have detection limits or residual baselines.',5,'GDC allowed','Determine',{check:{mode:'number',value:10,tolerance:1e-9}});
  add('Challenge','C24','A campaign gains \\(120\\) contacts in week \\(1\\) and grows by \\(8\\%\\) weekly for \\(18\\) weeks. Calculate the total and evaluate whether rounding each weekly term before summing is advisable.','Total \\(\\approx5519.54\\); retain guard digits and round the final result.','Using the finite-sum formula avoids repeated rounding. Weekly whole-person interpretation may be applied after the model total is calculated.',6,'GDC allowed','Calculate');

